<title>Introduction to an infectious disease model</title>
<p><ref>Video 1</ref> | <ref>Summary</ref> | <ref>Transcript</ref></p>

<section><title>Summary</title>

<p>A simple model consisting of three state variables is introduced to describe the behavior of an infectious disease modelled by a discrete dynamical system. These state variables are:
  <ul>
    <li><p><m>\textbf{S}</m>: <alert>the Susceptibles</alert></p>
      <p><m>S(t) =</m> the number of individuals at time <m>t</m> that can get infected</p></li>
    <li><p><m>\textbf{I}</m>: <alert>the Infectives</alert></p>
      <p><m>I(t) =</m> the number of individuals at time <m>t</m> who are infected and can transmit the disease</p></li>
    <li><p><m>\textbf{R}</m>: <alert>the Removed</alert></p>
      <p><m>R(t) =</m> the number of individuals at time <m>t</m> who are immune (or recovered)</p></li>
  </ul>
</p>

<p>The figure from the video shown below illustrates the relationship between the three state variables introduced.</p> 
<figure>
<image source="doenet:rXNsNcsATM5qGhgjDEaXwv" asfilename="Discrete SIR screenshot (1).png" size="large" mimeType="image/png" imageName="Infographic showing the interaction between the susceptibles, the infectives, and the removed" authorName="Virginia Mae" licenseCodes="CC-BY"><shortDescription>Infographic showing the interaction between the susceptibles, the infectives, and the removed</shortDescription></image>
<caption>Relationships between state variables in an infectious disease model</caption>
</figure>

<p>The two arrows in the figure represent two expressions that describe important changes to the dynamical system. These expressions are:
  <ol>
    <li><p>The rate at which susceptibles become infected at time <m>t</m>,</p>
    <p><m>S \longrightarrow I = b S I</m></p>
    <p>where <m>b</m> is an infection rate parameter that combines the effects of both the likelihood that a susceptible and an infected person will interact as well as the likelihood that such an interaction will lead to an infection.</p></li>

    <li><p>The rate at which infected individuals recover at time <m>t</m>,</p>
    <p><m>I \longrightarrow R = a I</m></p>
    <p>where <m>a</m> is a recovery rate parameter that captures how quickly an individual recovers from the disease.</p></li>
  </ol>
</p>

</section>